  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    The fire-cores Noctisum~ had brought as trade-gifts sat untouched on the council shelf, still wrapped in tunnel-leather, still radiating warmth that no one in the chamber wanted badly enough to acknowledge needing. He counted them. Seven. Enough to heat the Beacon Stack's lower galleries for a week, enough to remind every penguin present that the Long Dark was forty rotations away and the recharging adepts were still arguing about who had the authority to authorize their work. Seven cores. Seven days. The arithmetic was the only honest thing in the room.
    
    The eldest mystic had been dead six days by then, though no one used the word. *Transitioned*, they said. *Released.* As if Veil☽ Glow had simply decided to stop holding the light. Noctisum~ had seen the body — the iridescence fading from her feathers like dye washing out of cheap cloth — and had thought: *three rotations before the crystal shipments stop, four before someone blames us.* He'd been wrong. It took two.
    
    The council session was the fourth since the death, and the factions had calcified into something brittle and precise. Tide-speakers on the eastern ledge. Gift-keepers arrayed beneath the light-shaft. The Long-dark Edict's followers clustered near the exits, which Noctisum~ respected; always know your way out. And the harp — the Radiant Aurora-harp, that scarred and singing ruin — stood at the chamber's center on its bone-carved stand, catching light from the shaft above and throwing it in directions that didn't correspond to any geometry Noctisum~ trusted.
    
    He touched his amulet without thinking. The fire-core inside was cracked — had been cracked since the Orca Incursion, since the forge at Midway Shelf where seventeen skeletons still stood as lessons about what happens when you break the courier laws. The crack ran through the crystal's heart the way old grief runs through a penguin who keeps working: visible if you looked, functional if you didn't. It burned cold against his throat. The presences stirred at its edges — whisper-things that had no names but opinions about everything — and one of them said, in the mineral language of deep ice: *The light is wrong today.*
    
    He listened. He'd stopped pretending he didn't listen seasons ago. The presences were better economists than most merchants; they felt supply chains in the ice-memory the way others felt weather.
    
    A Tide-speaker was arguing that Veil☽ Glow's bloodline held succession rights. A Gift-keeper was quoting precedent from before the Orca Incursion. The harp hummed — not played, not touched, just *hummed* — and Noctisum~ watched three penguins flinch and two pretend they hadn't. Then Ice-Crowned Shexuwohua, stack-risen and furious, crossed the chamber floor to seize the harp's frame, and what happened next depends on what you think *seize* means. Noctisum~ saw a penguin grab an instrument too old to be grabbed. The harp screamed — a sound like aurora-glass shattering in reverse — and a new fracture line split its frame from the second-war crack to the base.
    
    Four penguins Walked that night. Just stood up from the memorial fires and moved toward the ice-edge with the slow certainty of water finding a drain. Noctisum~ watched from the trade-gallery and thought: *the surface is breaking.* Not metaphor. Observation. When your trading partners start walking into the dark, you adjust your supply estimates.
    
    He sent three coded messages through the Courier Corps before dawn. The couriers' ink-marked flippers glowed in the tunnel-light as they took his sealed crates. One message to ☽'voidoth in the deep warrens. One to Shadow-Broker Nyla's remnant network — what was left of it. One he doesn't discuss. The bones at Midway Shelf remember what happens to penguins who interfere with couriers; Noctisum~ remembers what happens to penguins who don't plan ahead.
    
    The crack was already there. He just stopped pretending the harp was whole.
    
    ---
    
    Xi was standing closest when the light changed.
    
    Not by accident. Xi had positioned themself behind the Gift-keepers' delegation — low enough to avoid the eye-lines of anyone who mattered, high enough to see the harp's strings when they caught the aurora-shaft's glow. They'd been watching the forty-seventh string, the slack one, the one that hadn't sounded in three seasons. It sounded.
    
    When Shexuwohua crossed the floor, Xi saw what the Nightshelf merchant couldn't have seen from his angle: the harp's strings were already vibrating before anyone touched it. The resonance had started during the Tide-speaker's third sentence — a low harmonic that Xi felt in the frostbite scars on their flipper, the ones Cold-flip Qing's cave-in had given them. The harp was *choosing*. And what it chose was fracture.
    
    The amulet at the Nightshelf merchant's throat — Xi had catalogued it the moment Noctisum~ entered the chamber. Cracked fire-core, yes. Old pain, perhaps. But the crack was too clean, too deliberatly placed. Xi had hollowed fire-cores before, dozens of them, threading aurora-crystal slivers into the gap where the heat used to be, creating hybrid stones that could pass through Nightshelf checkpoints as fire and through Aurora inspections as dead weight. Noctisum~'s amulet had that same surgical fracture. The merchant was carrying something in that crack, and it wasn't grief.
    
    The harp screamed. Xi heard what the others didn't: underneath the shatter-sound, a note. A single sustained frequency that matched the pitch of the aurora when it swings north during the Long Dark's deepest point — the note the old mystics called *the unworthy wind*. Xi had been blessed by that sound once, in the Pinnacle's vault, when Oeichowei insisted every member hear the harp before swearing the coded greeting. The blessing had tasted like blood-salt and sleeplessness. Xi hadn't slept clean since.
    
    But the note meant something. The harp was singing the unworthy out.
    
    Four penguins Walked that night, and Xi stood at the ice-edge watching them go and thought: *yes.* Not cruelty. Theology. The aurora calls home what doesn't belong; the harp is the aurora's throat; the Walking is the answer to a question the council was too fractured to ask. *Who among you is unfit?* Four answered. The rest should have listened.
    
    Xi wrapped three fire-cores in stolen Aurora Stack cloth that night — the small cruelty they allowed themself, the cloth's ceremonial thread pressing against crystal that would warm some Nightshelf gallery for a month. The smuggle-routes ran parallel to the courier paths but never crossed them. Xi wasn't stupid. The bones at Midway Shelf remembered the price of that particular miscalculation, and Xi remembered names the bones had forgotten.
    
    The Accord was already broken. Xi had simply stopped performing belief in it.
    
    What the harp sang confirmed what Xi had known since Veil☽ Glow's light went out: the council that let her die channeling was the same council that would let the crystals rot unstockpiled while the Long Dark swallowed everything. The Pinnacle was right to splinter. Oeichowei was right to gather the ones who chose. The harp's new fracture line was not damage. It was the crack that was already there, made visible.
    
    Xi sent no messages. Messages leave traces. Instead they adjusted the next smuggle-run's route to pass within sight of the Aerie Perch, where a nod and a flicker of the eyes would carry everything that needed carrying.
    
    ---
    
    We saw.
    
    We saw the Nightshelf merchant enter the council chamber wearing fire at his throat — a fire-core amulet, unsheathed, the glow visible through his charcoal wraps like an ember carried into a library. We noted this. We noted his position: western gallery, trade-observer's bench, equidistant from both exits. We noted his burns. We noted his pauses — mid-sentence stillnesses that suggested either calculation or something less stable, though we did not speculate.
    
    We saw Shexuwohua cross the floor. We saw the approach for what it was: not impulse but the final motion of a penguin who had decided three sessions ago and only now found the nerve. The harp stood at the chamber's center. Its frame bore two fracture lines — Mbra∴silentiu, the Breach — sealed but legible, the history of violence written in ice that would not forget. When Shexuwohua's flippers closed on the frame, we were already moving.
    
    We did not reach it in time. This is recorded. The harp's frame split — a third line, joining the old wounds, running from the second-war scar to the base with the precision of something that had been waiting. The sound it produced was significant. We will not characterize the sound, because characterization is interpretation, and what we offer is account.
    
    Lianchaifing hewei'Summit of our delegation secured the harp within the hour. We placed it in transit-wrapping and moved it to the Aerie Perch before the session formally adjourned. Custody was not disputed. Custody was *assumed*, because we were the only faction with a vault deep enough and a membership disciplined enough to protect what the old council had failed to protect. The Radiant Aurora-harp had survived three wars. It would not be lost to committee.
    
    The session's collapse confirmed what we had known since Veil☽ Glow's death exposed the council's rot: governance by consensus had become governance by paralysis. The Tide-speakers' bloodline claims were theater. The Gift-keepers' precedents were the precedents of a world that no longer existed. The Flipper Accord required crystal shipments to continue. Crystal shipments required recharging authority. Recharging authority required the harp. The harp required us.
    
    We determined this. It was clear.
    
    The Nightshelf merchant's seven fire-cores remained on the council shelf when we secured the chamber. We counted them. We left them. Let the record show we left them.
    
    ---
    
    Three days after the session, the courier called Splitpath carried a sealed crate from ☽'scar Ramtatus~ to the Aerie Perch. The crate's contents were logged as aurora-crystal samples for quality assessment. Splitpath's ink-marked flippers glowed in both light and dark as she passed the seventeen skeletons at Midway Shelf, and she did what every courier did: looked at the bones, felt her mind concentrate, kept walking.
    
    Noctisum~ waited in the deep warrens for ☽'voidoth's reply. The presences murmured about ice-memory disruptions near the Glow-Fissure — new corruption seeping through old breach-points — and he listened, adjusting his estimates. The Aurora Stack's crystal stockpiles were frozen by the deadlock. Forty rotations to the Long Dark. If the Pinnacle controlled recharging authority through the harp, the Nightshelf would negotiate with the Pinnacle. Simple arithmetic. He touched his amulet. The crack in the fire-core pulsed with something that was not heat.
    
    Xi adjusted their smuggle-route and passed the Aerie Perch at dusk. The nod was given. The flicker returned. Oeichowei's coded greeting carried between two penguins who had chosen and stayed and were still here, and the meaning required no words. Xi did not look toward the ice-edge where four sets of tracks still marked the snow, leading outward and never returning. The tracks were answer enough. The aurora would sort the rest.
    
    At the Aerie Perch, the Pinnacle placed the Radiant Aurora-harp in the deep vault. Its three fracture lines — old war, old war, new crisis — caught the stored light and threw shadows that moved without source. Lianchaifing hewei'Summit reported the vault sealed. The coded greeting passed through the corridors, nod to flicker to nod: *we chose, we stayed, we're still here.*
    
    Below them, unseen, the seven fire-cores still sat on the council shelf in the abandoned chamber, radiating warmth into a room where no one remained to need it. Splitpath's crate reached the Aerie Perch and was opened by flippers that recognized its true contents — not crystal samples but a manifest of Nightshelf tunnel conditions, the deep warrens' structural reports, the information a merchant trades when he's buying something more expensive than crystal.
    
    No one had won. The compromise leader — chosen for her willingness to end the argument, not her fitness to lead — took her position the following week, and the crystal shipments resumed at sixty percent capacity, and the Accord survived because extinction was the alternative. But the harp sat in the Pinnacle's vault. And Xi's smuggle-routes now carried fire-cores wrapped in ceremony-cloth past the bones at Midway Shelf. And Noctisum~ spoke to his presences in the dark and calculated which silence would cost less than which truth, and the presences answered in the mineral language of ice that remembers everything and forgives nothing.
    
    The crack was already there. It was always already there. Who was standing closest when the light changed depends on who you ask, and none of them are lying, and none of them are right.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771580811061_0", anchor: "since the Orca Incursion, since the forge at Midway Shelf where seventeen skeletons still stood", text: "The Orca Incursion has not happened yet. During the Faction Wars — the stated focal era — orcas are peripheral raiders. Vanishing crews, strange songs at the border colonies, ambient dread. Not a named war. Not a forge at Midway Shelf. Not seventeen skeletons standing as settled legend. Noctisum~ cannot carry personal trauma from an event two full eras in his future. This is source contamination: Incursion-era impressions bleed into Faction Wars strata throughout the eastern Foundation galleries. I have mapped the interleaving. The ice does not sort chronologically at those depths, and a chronicle drawn from ice-memory testimony will inherit whatever the ice has shuffled together. The chronicler did not invent this. The ice gave it to them out of order.", type: :temporal, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771580811061_1", anchor: "the forge at Midway Shelf where seventeen skeletons still stood as lessons about what happens when you break the courier laws", text: "I corrected this geography in my annotation on Noctisum~'s entry. The skeletons are at Midway Shelf. The forge was adjacent — forty body-lengths of open ice. This is the second text to compress them. The orca raids that made the Incursion what it was — coordinated shore-breaker assaults, herding formations, hunt-songs at bone-aching frequencies — turned Midway Shelf into an emergency staging point. The forge existed because penguins were dying faster than fire-cores could be distributed through normal channels. Compressing that distance erases why the forge was there at all.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771580811061_2", anchor: "whisper-things that had no names but opinions about everything", text: "There it is. Cold-burn perception, whisper-echoes from nothing, presences others cannot perceive. The chronicle frames this as useful — presences as economic advisors, ice-memory consultants. The canon term is Touched. Noctisum~ is functional but changed, and the cracked fire-core at his throat is the breach-point. That the chronicler renders this as competence rather than contamination tells you who wrote the account.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771580811061_3", anchor: "Not cruelty. Theology.", text: "No. The Walking is a compulsion, not a verdict. It seizes those who have lost much or suffered greatly. It is not the aurora sorting the unworthy. It is not an answer to a question the council failed to ask. Four penguins walked into the dark because grief or exhaustion or the thinning veil broke something in them, and Xi watched from the ice-edge and called it *confirmation*. Every faction, every era, finds a way to frame the suffering of others as evidence that their theology is correct. Xi's version is more honest than most only because it does not bother pretending to mourn first.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771580811061_4", anchor: "We will not characterize the sound, because characterization is interpretation, and what we offer is account", text: "Account. They seized the harp within the hour, moved it to their vault before the session adjourned, and called it custody rather than confiscation. This sentence is the Pinnacle performing objectivity the way a penguin performs grief at a rival's funeral — technically correct, structurally dishonest. I distrust every account written by the party holding the artifact.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771580811061_5", anchor: "new corruption seeping through old breach-points", text: "The Glow-Fissure is one of at least four active corruption source-points — the Still Pools, the Forgotten Shore, and the Veil Vents form the network's deeper structure. Cleansing rituals create temporary sanctified zones, but the sources remain active, and cults like the Communion deliberately break those sanctifications. That Noctisum~ receives this intelligence from his presences rather than from Nightshelf structural reports should alarm the reader more than the corruption itself. His information chain runs through the taint.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771580811061_6", anchor: "Its frame bore two fracture lines", text: "Two here. Three in the final section: 'old war, old war, new crisis.' The Pinnacle's own account says the third line was added by Shexuwohua's seizure. Either the narrator miscounts before the event they witnessed firsthand, or the second fracture line was not visible from the Pinnacle's vantage and they are reconstructing the harp's damage history from received accounts they did not bother to verify. For a faction that insists it offers 'account' rather than 'interpretation,' this is sloppy.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
